//! Checks ASERT (D-026) against the test vectors of independent implementations.
//!
//! 1. Bitcoin Cash Node's official aserti3-2d test vectors `tests/data/asert/run01`…`run12`, taken verbatim from
//!    <https://gitlab.com/bitcoin-cash-node/bchn-sw/qa-assets/-/tree/master/test_vectors/aserti3-2d>
//!    (qa-assets commit 1e153d75cdaaa21ca3988977e946a538c74751a2; the vectors specified by the aserti3-2d spec
//!    <https://github.com/bitcoincashorg/bitcoincash.org/blob/master/spec/2020-11-15-asert.md>, generated by BCHN's
//!    C++ reference implementation). Each line `iteration height time bits`: the child's bits when the parent has
//!    height `height` and timestamp `time`. The parameters are BCH mainnet's: T = 600, τ = 172800, the PoW limit is
//!    BCHN's powLimit 2^224 − 1. The files' SHA-256 digests are pinned in [`OFFICIAL`].
//! 2. `tests/data/asert_ref_vectors.txt`: generated by `tests/data/asert_ref.py` (arbitrary-precision Python
//!    implementing D-026 literally, which first validated itself against all the official vectors of 1), covering the
//!    ranges outside the reference implementation's preconditions: the devnet parameters (T = 5, τ = 600, limit
//!    0x207fffff ≈ 2^255), intermediate products above 256 bits, inputs that would overflow i128, the frac boundaries
//!    (0 / 65535) and the clamps at both ends (1 and the PoW limit), and the genesis-anchored [`Asert`].

use ksana_consensus::{
    asert_target, bits_from_target, genesis_header, pow_limit_target, target_from_bits, Asert,
    Consensus, ConsensusError, PowConsensus, U256,
};
use ksana_primitives::hash::sha256;
use ksana_primitives::{BlockHeader, BtcAnchor, Params, DEVNET};

